        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is proportion more important on a G+1 house elevation than on a taller home?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On a taller G+2 or G+3 building, minor proportion errors get absorbed into the overall height and are easy to overlook. On a single floor or G+1 home, every element — window size, roof overhang, plinth height — reads clearly because there is so little else competing for attention. This makes proportion the single most important design tool for a G+1 facade, since there is nowhere for a busy or cluttered design to hide."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a G+1 home afford a full metal facade, or only an accent panel?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Because a G+1 home has a smaller total wall area than a multi-storey house, it is often financially realistic to use a higher grade material across a larger proportion of the facade for a similar total budget. A single floor home can sometimes afford full metal cladding on the front facade for roughly what would only cover a partial accent on a three storey building, making engineered metal systems a strong fit for smaller homes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the most common mistake homeowners make with G+1 elevation design?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Adding tall thin columns or exaggerated pillars purely to make a single floor home look taller is the most common mistake, and it usually looks disproportionate rather than grand. Over-decorating a small facade with multiple textures and colours, and placing windows without a clear rhythm, are the other frequent issues on single floor homes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does a G+1 elevation design cost in India?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a typical single floor or G+1 home, elevation costs commonly range from ₹1.5 to ₹3 lakh for a plaster and paint based design with limited accents, up to ₹5 to ₹10 lakh or more where a significant portion of the front facade is finished in engineered metal panels or premium stone, priced roughly ₹800 to ₹2,500 per square foot for the metal accent area."
          }
        }
